          "why": "Added lower indices 5 + 6 without incrementing the upper index."
        },
        {
          "text": "$^{24}C_{11}$",
          "why": "Added upper and lower indices independently."
        }
      ],
      "solution_text": "By Pascal's identity $^{n}C_{r} + {^{n}C_{r+1}} = {^{n+1}C_{r+1}}$:\n$^{12}C_{5} + {^{12}C_{6}} = {^{13}C_{6}}$"
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "question_text": "If $^{n}C_{3} + {^{n}C_{4}} = {^{10}C_{4}}$, find the value of $n$.",
      "structure": "pascals-identity-solve-unknown",
      "mastery": false,
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "$9$",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "$10$",
          "why": "Equated n to the upper index of the sum without subtracting 1."
        },
        {
          "text": "$7$",
          "why": "Subtracted 3 from 10 instead of using Pascal's relation n + 1 = 10."
        },
        {
          "text": "$8$",
          "why": "Subtracted 2 instead of identifying n + 1 = 10."
        }
      ],
      "solution_text": "By Pascal's identity $^{n}C_{3} + {^{n}C_{4}} = {^{n+1}C_{4}}$:\n$n + 1 = 10$\n$n = 9$"
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "question_text": "If $^{11}C_{r} + {^{11}C_{r+1}} = {^{12}C_{6}}$, find the value of $r$.",
      "structure": "pascals-identity-solve-unknown",
      "mastery": false,
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "$5$",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "$6$",
          "why": "Selected r + 1 = 6 as the value of r without subtracting 1."
        },
        {
          "text": "$4$",
          "why": "Subtracted 2 from 6 instead of solving r + 1 = 6."
        },
        {
          "text": "$11$",
          "why": "Used the upper index 11 of the combinations on the left-hand side."
        }
      ],
      "solution_text": "By Pascal's identity $^{11}C_{r} + {^{11}C_{r+1}} = {^{12}C_{r+1}}$:\n$r + 1 = 6$\n$r = 5$"
